## INTRODUCTION
Since the first report on metal-organic framework (MOF) based white light emission (WLE) in 2009,1 many efforts have been made to improve the WLE performance via changing the MOF structure and composition. $2{-}5$ So far, WLE has been generated based on four types of emission sources: 1) organic ligands;6-10 2) metal nodes, or metal nodes combined with ligands;11-13 3) encapsulated guest molecules;14-18 and 4) organic ligands combined with guests materials.19 Among these categories, MOF-guest-based systems show some advantages over others, where both metal centers and ligands are not required to be emissive so the selection of MOFs is expanded. In these systems, MOFs serve as a scaffold for the dye guests to prevent the aggregation caused quenching (ACQ). Typically, the MOF-guest composites are synthesized using a two-step process, where a pristine MOF is synthesized first, and then selected dyes are incorporated into MOFs via ion exchange.14-15, 20-22 These dyes@MOF composites often exhibit great WLE performance; however, this approach has certain limitations, for example, the size of the dyes must be small enough to fit into the aperture of the MOF pores. Also, the leakage of the dye molecules is another issue if the host-guest interactions are not sufficiently strong. In addition, the interaction between different dyes, such as fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET), could interfere with the WLE and reduce its efficiency.
Scheme 1.The schematic diagrams of three models for the preparation of dye and ZIF-8 based composites for white light emission.
A recent study shows that if different dye molecules are encapsulated into MOF at different locations, bright WLE with a CIE (0.35, 0.32) could be achieved.16 The control of guest location in this work is achieved via a core-shell cyclodextrin-based MOF, in which the time-consuming synthesis and the microsized crystal could limit the device fabrication and commercial applications. As such further improvement in developing dyes@MOF based systems with facile synthesis route, high processability, and excellent WLE performance is much needed.
Herein, we present a facile approach to encapsulate multiple dyes into zeolitic imidazolate framework 8 (ZIF-8) during the crystal formation. In order to understand and control the effect of dye locations on WLE, three models are established, namely multi-phase single-shell dye@ZIF-8 (Model 1), single-phase single-shell dyes@ZIF-8 (Model 2) and single-phase multi-shell dyes $@$ ZIF-8 (Model 3) (Scheme 1).23 We hypothesize that the WLE can be achieved in all three models by changing the components and concentrations of dyes molecules and the multi-shell structure in Model $^3$ can eliminate the interactions between different dye molecules by placing them in different shells, which represents an entirely new design. $24^{-25}$ The reasons for choosing ZIF-8 as the host MOF are three-fold: (1) ZIF-8 can be easily synthesized in water and organic solvent at room temperature, which allows a broader selection of dyes; $26-27$ (2) the size and shape control of ZIF-8 nanocrystals is reported, which allows us to have desirable nanocrystals for solutionbased  fabrication of WLE devices; and $\left(3\right)$ encapsulation of multiple guest species inside the ZIF-8 with controlled locations has been established.23, 28
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
To demonstrate our proof-of-concept design, three dye molecules were chosen as encapsulates. They emit three different colors, namely red (rhodamine B or RB), green (fluorescein or F) and blue (7-amino-4- (trifluoromethyl)coumarin or C-151). A careful analysis was made to insure a good match between the ZIF-8 pore structure and the dimensions of these dye molecules. As shown in Figure 1, the aperture of the pore window and the cage size of ZIF-8 are 3.40 Å and 11.6 Å,29 respectively. Therefore, the selected dyes should be larger than the aperture size but at a similar length scale as the cavity in order to be securely encapsulated within the ZIF-8 pores. The molecular sizes of RB, F, and C-151 are ${\sim}10.2{\times}16.8$ Å, ${\bf u.o}{\bf{\times}}{\bf{1}}3.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and $8.0{\times}11.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure 1b-d), $30{-}31$ respectively, well matching the requirement.
The excellent emission properties of $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}15^{1}{\ @}\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8^{2}$ , $\mathrm{F@ZIF}-8^{2}$ , and RB@ZIF-82 prompted us to evaluate the possibility of combining the three colors to achieve WLE by simply mixing these three composites. As expected, the WLE was realized by optimizing the ratio of C-151@ZIF-82, ${\mathrm{F@ZIF-}}$ 82 and RB@ZIF-82. For example, when a mixture of 0.0137 ${\bf w t\%}$ C-151@ZIF-82, 0.0146 $w t\%$ $\mathrm{F@ZIF}-8^{2}$ and $0.0372\mathrm{wt}\%$ RB $@$ ZIF-82 with mass ratio 2:1:3 was grinded, a WLE was indeed achieved as shown in Figure 2g. The corresponding CIE coordinates of (0.32, 0.34) were calculated (Figure S5), which are very close to the pure white color of (0.33, 0.33). These results indicate that single-shell dye@ZIF-82 nanocrystals can be combined to form multi-phase phosphors of targeted colors. In addition, due to nano-size of dye@ZIF-82, they can be well dispersed in solution, making them highly solution processable for device fabrication (Figure $S_{7}$ ).
The above results inspired us to explore Model 2 (Scheme 1). Would encapsulation of red, green, and blue emitting dyes simultaneously in ZIF-8 lead to a single-phase C151&F&RB@ZIF-82, for which WLE color may be tuned and optimized by adjusting the concentrations of encapsulated C-151, F, and RB? Also, by combining all the dyes in one nanocrystal, we can optimize the emission property in a more direct way and simplify the fabrication process. To answer this question, we applied Model 2 to synthesize single-phase single-shell C-151&F&RB@ZIF-82 composite by adding C-151, F, and RB simultaneous into precursor solutions of ZIF-8. As expected, by carefully adjusting the concentration of three dyes, white emitting C
151&F&RB@ZIF-82 composites were obtained. For example, using 20 uL $5~\mathrm{mM}$ C-151, 40 uL $5^{\mathrm{~mMF}}$ and 20 uL $5\mathrm{mM}$ RB (Figure 3a, black line) or 20 uL 5 mM C-151, 20 uL 5 mM F and 40 uL $5~\mathrm{mM}$ RB (Figure 3a, red line) for the synthesis, both of nanocrystals led to emission spectra that covers the visible light region $\left(400{-}650\quad\mathrm{nm}\right)$ with WLE. The corresponding CIE chromaticity coordinates, (0.30, 0.34) and (0.34, 0.34), are both very close to the pure white light (Figure 3b), which demonstrates that Model 2 also can be employed as a strategy to realize WLE by combing three dyes together under optimized conditions. However, because of the strong fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) between these three dyes (Figure S8), it is difficult to calculate their accurate concentrations upon mixing them together.
As mentioned above, one major drawback of Model 2 is the energy transfer between the emissive dye molecules, which cannot be ignored as the efficiency of WLE might be significantly decreased by this kind of interactions. For C-151, F and RB, the strong FRET was found in their binary mixture solutions (Figure S8). To avoid this, we propose to place different dyes at different locations (shells) of a nanocrystal. It was found in a previous study that it is possible to encapsulate different guest molecules into a multi-shell ZIF-8 nanocrystal with sufficiently large spatial separation, in which the FRET between two guests can be totally suppressed by the solid ZIF-8 shell.23 Therefore, we developed Model 3, a single-phase multi-shell dyes@ZIF-82 (Scheme 1), where the three dyes are encapsulated consecutively into ZIF-8 nanocrystals via shell-by-shell overgrowth and separated by solid ZIF-8 shells. As shown in Figure 4a, the first step is to encapsulate two dyes (in any combination) into multi-shell ZIF-8 during the crystal formation, where the emission color of multi-shell dyes@ZIF-82 can be easily tuned from pink, yellow to cyan by formation of $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8^{2}@\mathrm{C}-151@\mathrm{ZIF}-8^{2}$ 2, $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{2}@\mathrm{F}@\mathrm{ZIF-}$ 82 and $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}{151}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8^{2}@\mathrm{F}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8^{2}$ , respectively. As one example to confirm our hypothesis, $\mathrm{C-151}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8^{2}$ was used as the core to perform the overgrowth of $\mathrm{F@ZIF-8}$ with increased F concentrations, and the so formed $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}151@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8^{2}@\mathrm{F}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8^{2}$ was then used as the new core for further overgrowth of $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ to prepare the multi-shell dyes@ZIF-82 nanocomposites for WLE (Figure S9 and Figure 4b).
PXRD patterns of simulated ZIF-8, C-151@ZIF-82, F@ZIF-82, $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8^{2}$ , C-151&F&RB@ZIF-82 and $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}151@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8^{\circ}@\mathrm{F}@\mathrm{ZIF}.$ 一 $8^{2}@\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8^{2}$ .
As shown in Figure 4b and 4c, under the optimized synthesis conditions, the CIE chromaticity coordinates of multi-shell $\mathrm{C-151}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{2}@\mathrm{F@ZIF-}8^{2}@\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{2}$ changed from (0.21, 0.26) to (0.32, 0.34) with the increasing RB concentration, suggesting that Model 3 can also be very efficient for preparing composite materials with systematically tunable emission colors, including white light. The multi-shell structures formation was further proved by scanning electron microscope (SEM) as depicted in Figure 4d. Via sequential shell-by-shell overgrowth in water and methanol, it is observed that the size of ZIF-8 gradually increased accompanying with the morphology switched between truncated cube and truncated rhombic dodecahedron.  The growth sequence and the thickness of shells did not have obvious effect on the light quality. The emission spectrum and CIE chromaticity coordinates of the white emitting $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{\circ}@\mathrm{F@ZIF-}8^{\circ}@\mathrm{C-}151@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{\circ}$ multishell nanocomposite (Figure S10a) are very similar to those of $\mathrm{C-151}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{2}@\mathrm{F@ZIF-}8^{2}@\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{2}$ (Figure 4b). In addition, very little changes were observed in the fluorescence of $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{\circ}@\mathrm{F@ZIF-}8^{\circ}@\mathrm{C-}151@\mathrm{ZIF-}8^{\circ}$ samples with different out-shell thickness of ZIF-8 (Figure S10b).
The PXRD patterns of single-phase single-shell dye@ZIF-82, multi-phase single-shell dyes@ZIF-82 and single-phase multi-shell dyes@ZIF-82 remain essentially the same as that of the simulated ZIF-8 (Figure 4e), confirming that the in-situ encapsulated dyes have no effect on the single crystal structure of the pristine ZIF-8. Leakage checking experiments confirmed that dyes were well trapped within the ZIF-8 pores. All samples tested showed no trace of leaking (Figure S11).
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we have developed a facile approach to prepare dyes@MOF-based host-guest nanocomposites to generate white light emission via in-situ encapsulation of dyes into ZIF-8 pores during its crystal formation. We have studied three models, in which the dye locations are tunable. To avoid energy transfer caused efficiency decrease due to the interactions between encapsulated dye molecules, a single-phase multi-shell dyes@ZIF-8 nanocomposite could confine and isolate different dye molecules in different ZIF-8 shells. By adjusting the composition and concentration of encapsulated dyes, the dyes@ZIF-8 composites prepared based on the three models all show high WLE quality. Combining the facile and scalable synthesis, the use of inexpensive and commercially available dyes, and systematic color tunability, this study has offered a novel and efficient approach for making highly solution-processable dyes@MOFs-based nanocomposite materials that are promising candidate for use as a new family of phosphors in energy-efficient lighting devices.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
General Information. The commercial chemicals were used with further purification and a detailed list of chemicals can be found in the Supporting Information.
Instruments. The fluorescence spectra were measured using fluoromax-4 spectrofluorometer, Horiba Scientific. The quantum yield was obtained using absolute PL quantum yield spectrometer C11347. Powder X-ray diffraction patterns were measured using Ultima IV X-ray diffractometer with $\mathtt{C u}$ Kα radiation. Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were obtained on JEOL JSM6340F.
## Synthesis of dye@ZIF-8
The dye@ZIF-8 was synthesized using two different methods (method A and B), where method A has much higher yield than method B, but method B shows better morphology and size control than method A for the synthesis of multi-shell ZIF-8.
## Synthesis of ZIF-8 (Method A)
${\boldsymbol5}\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution containing 2.72 M 2-methylimidazole and 0.55 mM CTAB was stirred for 1 minute, followed by addition of $5\mathrm{mL}$ 300 mg $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution. The mixture was stirred for another 10 seconds and then was left undisturbed for 2 hours at room temperature. The synthesized ${\mathrm{R6G}}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}$ -8was spun down at 9000 rpm for 10 minutes and washed twice using methanol.32
Synthesis of $\mathbf{C-151}@\mathbf{ZIF-8}$ (0.0040, 0.0137, 0.0171 and 0.0217 $\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) (Method A)
$5\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution containing 2.72 M 2-methylimidazole and 0.55 mM CTAB was stirred for 1 minute, followed by addition of 5mL 300 mg $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution. After the mixture was stirred for 10 seconds, different amount of $5\mathrm{mM}$ C-151 methanol solution (0.10 mL, 0.37 mL, 0.50 mL and $\mathbf{o.75~mL}$ ) was added. The mixture was stirred for another 10 seconds and then was left undisturbed for 2 hours at room temperature. The synthesized ${\mathrm{R6G@}}\varnothing{\mathrm{ZIF}}.$ 8was spun down at 9000 rpm for 10 minutes and washed twice using methanol.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{F@ZIF-8}$ (0.0030, 0.0072, 0.0146 and 0.0180 $\mathbf{wt\%}^{\prime}$ ) (Method A)
${\boldsymbol5}\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution containing 2.72 M $_2$ -methylimidazole and 0.55 mM CTAB was stirred for 1 minute, followed by addition of 5mL 300 mg $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution. After the mixture was stirred for 10seconds, different amount of $5^{\mathrm{~mM~F~}}$ in a solution containing 2.72 M 2-methylimidazole and 0.55 mM CTAB (0.10 mL, $\mathbf{0.25~mL}$ , $\mathbf{o.75~mL}$ and $\mathbf{1.0omL}$ ) was added. The mixture was stirred for another 10 seconds and then was left undisturbed for 2 hours at room temperature. The synthesized R6G@ZIF-8was spun down at 9000 rpm for 10 minutes and washed twice using methanol.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{RB}@\mathbf{ZIF-8}$ (0.0132, 0.0372, 0.0705 and 0.0803 $\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) (Method A)
$5\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution containing 2.72 M $^2$ -methylimidazole and 0.55 mM CTAB was stirred for 1 minute, followed by addition of 5mL 300 mg $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution. After the mixture was stirred for 10seconds, different amount of $5\mathrm{mM}$ RB aqueous solution (0.10 mL, 0.25 mL, $\mathbf{o.50~mL}$ and $\mathbf{o.75~mL}$ ) was added. The mixture was stirred for another 10 seconds and then was left undisturbed for 2 hours at room temperature. The synthesized ${\mathsf{R G G@Z I F-}}$ 8was spun down at 9000 rpm for 10 minutes and washed twice using methanol.
## Synthesis of dye@ZIF-8@ZIF-8 (dye@ZIF-82)
60 mL 30 mM 2-methylimidazole was used to re-disperse the as-synthesized dye@ZIF-8 and then60 mL 30 mM $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ methanol solution was added. After shaking 10 seconds, the mixture solution was left undisturbed at room temperature for 1.5 hours, and the formed dye@ZIF-82 was spun down at 6000 rpm for 10 minutes.28
## Synthesis of C-151&F&RB@ZIF-8 (Method A)
5mL aqueous solution containing 2.72 M 2-methylimidazole and 0.55 mM CTAB was stirred for 1 minute, followed by addition of 5mL 300 mg $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution. After the mixture was stirred for 10 seconds, a mixture solution of C-151, F and RB was added. The mixture was stirred for another 10 seconds and then was left undisturbed for 2 hours at room temperature. The synthesized C-151&F&R6G@ZIF-8 was spun down at 9000 rpm for 10 minutes and washed twice using methanol (one example for white light emission: 0.25 mL 0.5 mM C-151, 0.1 mL 0.5 mM F and 0.05 mL $\mathbf{0.5\PimM}$ RB).
## Synthesis of C-151&F&RB@ZIF-8 (Method B)
$\mathrm{\17.5~mL}$ solution of 790 mM 2-methylimidazole and 0.275 mM CTAB was stirred at 800 rpm for 5 minutes, followed by addition of 2.5 mL 97.5 mM $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution. After the mixture was stirred for $^3$ minutes, a mixture solution of C-151, F and RB was added and the solution was continued to stir for 2 minutes. Then the solution was left undisturbed for 3 hours at room temperature. The synthesized C-151&F&RB $@$ ZIF-8 composites were spun down at 8000 rpm for 10 minutes, washed twice using methanol and then dispersed in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol. (one example for white light emission: 20 uL 5 mM C-151, 40 uL $5\mathrm{mM}$ F and 20 uL $5\mathrm{mM}$ RB).
## Synthesis of rhodamine C-151&F&RB@ZIF-82
C-151&F&RB $@$ ZIF-82 was synthesized using the same method as the synthesis of dye $@$ ZIF-82.
## Synthesize of C-151@ZIF-8 (Method B)
$\mathrm{\mathbf{1.75}~m L}$ solution of $790~\mathrm{mM}$ 2-methylimidazole and $\mathbf{0.55\PimM}$ CTAB was stirred for 5 minutes, followed by addition of 0.25 mL 97.5 mM $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution. After the mixture was stirred for $^3$ minutes, 0.2 mL 0.5 mM C-151 methanol solution was added and the mixture was continued to stir for 2 minutes. Then the solution was left undisturbed for $^3$ hours at room temperature. The synthesized $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-151}@\mathbf{ZIF-}$ 8 composites were spun down at 8000 rpm for 10 minutes, washed twice using methanol and then dispersed in methanol.28
$\mathrm{F@ZIF-8}$ and $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ were synthesized using the same method as C-151@ZIF-8.
## Synthesis of C-151@ZIF-82
As-synthesized C-151@ZIF-8 composite was added into 15 mL methanol solution containing 30 mM 2-methylimidazole. After shaking for 5 seconds, 15 mL 30 mM $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ methanol solution was added. Then solution was left undisturbed at room temperature for 1 hour, and the formed C-151@ZIF-82 was spun down at 5000 rpm for 10 minutes.
## Synthesis of $\mathbf{C-151}@\mathbf{ZIF-8^{2}}@\mathbf{F}@\mathbf{ZIF-8}$ (Method B)
As-synthesized $\mathrm{C-151}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8^{2}$ was re-dispersed in 7.0 mL solution of 790 mM 2-methylimidazole and 0.41 mM CTAB. The solution was stirred for 5 minutes and then 1.0 mL 97.5 mM $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ aqueous solution was added. After the mixture was stirred for $30$ seconds, various amount of $5\mathrm{mM}$
F solution was added and the mixture was continued to stir for 2 minutes. Then the solution was left undisturbed for 1 hour at room temperature. The synthesized $\mathrm{C-151}@\mathrm{ZIF-}$ ${8^{2}@\mathsf{F@Z I F}{-}8}$ composites were spun down at 4000 rpm for 10 minutes, washed twice using methanol。
## Synthesis of $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-151}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{IF}\mathbf{-8^{2}@F@}\mathbf{ZIF}\mathbf{-8^{2}}$
As-synthesized $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}{15}{1}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8^{2}@\mathrm{F}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ was re-dispersed in 30 mL 30 mM 2-methylimidazole methanol solution. After shaking for 5 seconds, 30 mL 30 mM $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ methanol solution was added. Then solution was left undisturbed at room temperature for 1 hour, and the formed dye@ZIF-82 was spun down at $3500$ rpm for 10 minutes.
$\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF}–8^{2}@\mathrm{F}@\mathrm{ZIF}–8^{2}$ and $\mathrm{RB}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8^{2}@\mathrm{C}\lnot151@\mathrm{ZIF}-8^{2}$ were synthesized using the same method as $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}151@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8^{\circ}@\mathrm{F}@\mathrm{ZIF}.$ 82.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{C-151}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{IF-8^{2}}@\mathbf{F}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{IF-8^{2}}@\mathbf{RB}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{IF-8^{2}}$
$\mathrm{C-151}@Z\mathrm{IF}\mathrm{-}8^{\mathrm{2}}@\mathrm{F@ZIF}\mathrm{-}8^{\mathrm{2}}@\mathrm{RB}@$ ZIF-82 was synthesized using the same method as the synthesis of C-151 $@$ ZIF- $8^{2}@\mathrm{F}@$ ZIF82.
